The reference generator that the CSIPHY needs is powered off

The SM7150 vendor device tree (sdmmagpie-camera.dtsi) feeds each CSIPHY with TWO
regulators:

    regulator-names = "gdscr", "refgen";
    gdscr-supply = <&titan_top_gdsc>;
    refgen-supply = <&refgen>;

Mainline only has the first one. The camss node declares NO supply at all, and
the ones the driver asks for by name -"vdda-phy" and "vdda-pll"- do not exist in
the device tree, so regulator_bulk_get() returns dummy regulators and enabling
them does absolutely nothing.

refgen IS in mainline, but only the two DSI controllers reference it. Read on
the phone:

    refgen                    0    1      0 unknown     0mV
        ae94000.dsi-refgen    0

Use count 0 and 0 mV: off.

WHY THIS IS EXACTLY THE SYMPTOM
-------------------------------
refgen provides the reference voltage to the analogue receivers. In D-PHY the
two signalling modes do not need it in the same way:

    low power (LP)  -  rail to rail, ~1,2 V. Detected WITHOUT a reference.
    high speed (HS) -  ~200 mV differential over a ~200 mV common mode.
                       WITHOUT a reference it is never resolved.

And that is point by point what was measured throughout the investigation:

    the five lanes leave rest on emit           <- that is LP, it works
    the PHY interrupts at the frame rate        <- that is LP, it works
    the CSID does not receive a single sync     <- that is HS, it does not work
    no packets, no bad CRCs, no MISR, no errors <- there is nothing to forward

And it explains the dismissals that fitted nothing:

    - sweeping the settle count from 12 to 44 gave the IDENTICAL PHY state byte
      for byte. Of course: the count decides WHEN to sample something that is
      never resolved.
    - sweeping the link rate, the lane count (2, 3 and 4), the PHY selector and
      the polarity did not move a bit either.
    - the REAR camera, on a different CSIPHY, with a different sensor, a
      different driver, a different lane count and a different rate, also gives
      zero.

And it explains why the sdm845 DOES capture in mainline with this same code: its
CSIPHY is v1.0/v1.1, and the SM7150 one is v1.2. The sdm845 vendor device tree
does not ask for refgen for the CSIPHY; the SM7150 one does.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES
--------------------
The minimum needed to decide the hypothesis with ONE single variable: leave
refgen always on. If an image appears with this, the right thing afterwards is
to declare it as a CSIPHY supply -refgen-supply in the camss node and
{ .supply = "refgen" } in csiphy_res_7150- so that it is only turned on when the
camera uses it.
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7150.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7150.dtsi
@@ -1892,6 +1892,17 @@
 		refgen: regulator@ff1000 {
 			compatible = "qcom,sm7150-refgen-regulator",
 				     "qcom,sdm845-refgen-regulator";
 			reg = <0x0 0x00ff1000 0x0 0x60>;
+
+			/*
+			 * The CSIPHY v1.2 needs this reference to resolve
+			 * the high-speed differential signalling. In
+			 * mainline only the DSIs reference it, and it stayed
+			 * off: use count 0, 0 mV. Without it, the PHY sees the
+			 * low-power transitions -which are rail to
+			 * rail- but does not lock a single sync, and nothing at all
+			 * reaches the CSID.
+			 */
+			regulator-always-on;
 		};

 		mc_virt: interconnect@1380000 {
